// A three-state condition is refused only on the evaluation where it is
// actually unknown. `u || i < 2` is true on its first two evaluations and would
// be unknown on the third; this loop leaves before the third, so it must run to
// completion and print. A guard that refused as soon as it saw a three-state
// condition, rather than an unknown value, would stop this program.
// (`t_while_unknown_after_iterations` is the same loop without the break; its
// output before the refusal is not asserted, so this fixture carries that half.)
u: bool = ?
i: t64 = 0
while (u || i < 2) {
    i += 1
    print(i)
    if i == 2 { break }
}
print(99)
